1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,189
Welcome to chapter eight of my study series on Build Your First LLM by Hasan Degismez.

2
00:00:06,189 --> 00:00:11,575
Today we build the tokenizer, the piece that turns text into numbers and back.

3
00:00:11,575 --> 00:00:26,077
We'll write a character tokenizer and a word tokenizer from scratch, understand byte pair encoding, train our own BPE tokenizer on Shakespeare, and then use the production tokenizers, tiktoken and Hugging Face.

4
00:00:26,077 --> 00:00:36,574
I ran the whole notebook, and this chapter has more printed outputs that don't match a real run than any chapter so far, so I'll flag each one as we go.

5
00:00:37,424 --> 00:00:41,466
Last chapter we produced a clean dataset in JSON lines.

6
00:00:41,466 --> 00:00:44,331
Today we turn that text into token IDs.

7
00:00:44,331 --> 00:00:47,932
Next chapter, those IDs become embedding vectors.

8
00:00:47,932 --> 00:00:53,296
Everything follows the chapter eight notebook, linked in the description.

9
00:00:54,146 --> 00:00:55,290
Here's the plan.

10
00:00:55,290 --> 00:00:56,935
Why we tokenize at all.

11
00:00:56,935 --> 00:01:04,372
Then three strategies in rising sophistication: characters, words, and subwords with byte pair encoding.

12
00:01:04,372 --> 00:01:11,953
We'll train BPE ourselves, then use the real tools, then look at the quirks that affect prompts and costs.

13
00:01:11,953 --> 00:01:14,455
Finally we connect to chapter nine.

14
00:01:15,305 --> 00:01:16,127
A few terms.

15
00:01:16,127 --> 00:01:18,662
A token is a unit of text with an ID.

16
00:01:18,662 --> 00:01:22,087
The vocabulary is every token the tokenizer knows.

17
00:01:22,087 --> 00:01:25,580
Encoding goes from text to IDs, decoding goes back.

18
00:01:25,580 --> 00:01:30,169
Special tokens mark structure: padding, unknown, beginning and end.

19
00:01:30,169 --> 00:01:36,813
BPE, byte pair encoding, learns subword pieces by merging the most frequent pair again and again.

20
00:01:36,813 --> 00:01:44,554
And the round trip test, decode of encode equals the original text, is the first thing to check on any tokenizer.

21
00:01:45,403 --> 00:01:46,145
Part one.

22
00:01:46,145 --> 00:01:47,217
Why tokenize?

23
00:01:48,067 --> 00:01:50,000
Models only process numbers.

24
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:57,113
Back in chapter five, when we typed the secret to building AI is, GPT-2 actually received six integers.

25
00:01:57,113 --> 00:02:09,958
The tokenizer encodes text into those IDs, the embedding layer turns them into vectors, the transformer processes them, and the output is a probability for every token in the vocabulary.

26
00:02:09,958 --> 00:02:13,825
Then the tokenizer decodes the chosen ID back into text.

27
00:02:13,825 --> 00:02:17,486
It's the front door and the back door of every model.

28
00:02:18,335 --> 00:02:21,731
Special tokens aren't words, they're structure.

29
00:02:21,731 --> 00:02:23,464
Unknown is the fallback.

30
00:02:23,464 --> 00:02:32,710
Beginning of sequence and end of sequence mark boundaries, and end of sequence is how a model signals that it's done generating.

31
00:02:32,710 --> 00:02:37,406
Padding fills short sequences so a batch has a rectangular shape.

32
00:02:37,406 --> 00:02:50,842
GPT-2 is a good real example: it uses a single token, end of text, ID fifty thousand two hundred and fifty six, for both beginning and end, and it has no padding token at all by default.

33
00:02:51,691 --> 00:02:52,345
Part two.

34
00:02:52,345 --> 00:02:56,555
The simplest possible tokenizer: one character, one token.

35
00:02:57,405 --> 00:03:01,841
Here's the character tokenizer from the notebook, trimmed of comments.

36
00:03:01,841 --> 00:03:06,277
Two dictionaries, one for each direction, so both lookups are instant.

37
00:03:06,277 --> 00:03:12,676
Fit takes the set of characters, which removes duplicates, and sorts it so the IDs are deterministic.

38
00:03:12,676 --> 00:03:16,478
Encode is a list comprehension that looks each character up.

39
00:03:16,478 --> 00:03:22,941
Decode joins the characters back with an empty string, because characters have no spaces between them.

40
00:03:23,791 --> 00:03:26,806
Fitting on hello world gives ten characters.

41
00:03:26,806 --> 00:03:34,275
Because we sorted them, space and punctuation come first: their Unicode code points are smaller than letters.

42
00:03:34,275 --> 00:03:39,345
Hello encodes to three, five, six, six, seven, and decodes back perfectly.

43
00:03:39,345 --> 00:03:45,992
One thing to notice: this encode has no fallback, so a character it never saw raises a key error.

44
00:03:45,992 --> 00:03:51,610
Production character tokenizers either include every byte or add an unknown token.

45
00:03:52,460 --> 00:03:54,336
Here's the first book check.

46
00:03:54,336 --> 00:04:03,648
The notebook's sentence, tokenization is the first step in any language model, is fifty three characters with twenty two unique characters.

47
00:04:03,648 --> 00:04:06,528
The book prints fifty four and twenty four.

48
00:04:06,528 --> 00:04:15,706
The quick brown fox sentence later on is forty three characters with twenty eight unique ones, where the book says forty four and twenty.

49
00:04:15,706 --> 00:04:24,348
These are small slips, and the lesson is unchanged: at character level, every character is one token, so sequences get long fast.

50
00:04:25,197 --> 00:04:32,189
Pros: a tiny vocabulary, no unknowns as long as every character was seen, and it's trivial to write.

51
00:04:32,189 --> 00:04:43,514
Cons: very long sequences, the model has to learn how letters form words, and more tokens means more compute, since attention grows with the square of the length.

52
00:04:43,514 --> 00:04:51,694
A three thousand character essay would be three thousand tokens, and GPT-2 can only see one thousand and twenty four.

53
00:04:52,543 --> 00:04:53,315
Part three.

54
00:04:53,315 --> 00:04:56,192
The other extreme: whole words as tokens.

55
00:04:57,041 --> 00:05:02,188
The word tokenizer lowercases, splits on whitespace, and counts words.

56
00:05:02,188 --> 00:05:06,158
IDs zero to three are reserved for the special tokens.

57
00:05:06,158 --> 00:05:14,097
Then it keeps only the most frequent words, enough to fill the maximum vocabulary size, starting at ID four.

58
00:05:14,097 --> 00:05:16,817
Everything rarer will become unknown.

59
00:05:17,667 --> 00:05:25,287
Encoding uses dot get with a default of one, so an unknown word becomes the unknown token instead of crashing.

60
00:05:25,287 --> 00:05:29,444
Optionally we wrap the sequence in beginning and end tokens.

61
00:05:29,444 --> 00:05:35,609
Decoding looks up each ID, optionally skips the structural tokens, and joins with spaces.

62
00:05:35,609 --> 00:05:42,051
Notice the round trip is already lossy: we lowercased, and any unknown word is gone for good.

63
00:05:42,901 --> 00:05:47,489
Here's the real output on the cat, mat example with ten slots.

64
00:05:47,489 --> 00:05:51,781
The book shows mat before on, and dog as the last word in.

65
00:05:51,781 --> 00:05:56,887
In my run, on comes before mat, and was takes the last slot, not dog.

66
00:05:56,887 --> 00:05:57,183
Why?

67
00:05:57,183 --> 00:06:05,396
Was and dog each appear once, and Counter's most common breaks ties by first appearance, and was appears first.

68
00:06:05,396 --> 00:06:18,863
Also notice the token mat with a full stop attached: plain split doesn't separate punctuation, which is why the book suggests a regex like backslash b, backslash w plus, backslash b.

69
00:06:19,713 --> 00:06:24,488
The next example in the book isn't in the notebook, so I ran it myself.

70
00:06:24,488 --> 00:06:29,936
With a vocabulary of ten, the book says three of the five test words are unknown.

71
00:06:29,936 --> 00:06:30,608
I get one.

72
00:06:30,608 --> 00:06:44,596
With only six word slots, learning and of come first because they appear twice, and the remaining slots go, by first appearance, to neural, networks, learn and from, which happen to be exactly our test words.

73
00:06:44,596 --> 00:06:46,009
Only data is unknown.

74
00:06:46,009 --> 00:06:54,011
The lesson: on a tiny corpus, the most frequent list is mostly tie breaking, so be careful drawing conclusions from it.

75
00:06:54,861 --> 00:06:57,484
Side by side on the quick brown fox.

76
00:06:57,484 --> 00:07:01,710
Characters: vocabulary twenty eight, sequence forty three.

77
00:07:01,710 --> 00:07:08,705
Words: vocabulary twelve, eight distinct words plus four special tokens, and a sequence of nine.

78
00:07:08,705 --> 00:07:13,367
Notice the repeats ID four for both thes, because we lowercased.

79
00:07:13,367 --> 00:07:16,282
Words are about five times shorter here.

80
00:07:16,282 --> 00:07:28,085
But a word vocabulary keeps growing with every new name, typo, number and language, and each entry costs a row in the embedding table we'll build in chapter nine.

81
00:07:28,085 --> 00:07:29,906
Neither extreme is right.

82
00:07:30,756 --> 00:07:31,548
Part four.

83
00:07:31,548 --> 00:07:37,327
Subwords and byte pair encoding, the middle ground every modern LLM uses.

84
00:07:38,177 --> 00:07:41,121
Here are the three strategies on one word.

85
00:07:41,121 --> 00:07:43,644
Character level gives twelve tokens.

86
00:07:43,644 --> 00:07:51,003
Word level gives one token, but only if tokenization is in a huge vocabulary, and otherwise it's unknown.

87
00:07:51,003 --> 00:07:57,801
Subword gives two meaningful pieces, token and ization, which I confirmed with GPT-4's tokenizer.

88
00:07:57,801 --> 00:08:05,090
A small side note: the book says tokenization is thirteen characters in one place and twelve in another.

89
00:08:05,090 --> 00:08:05,932
It's twelve.

90
00:08:06,781 --> 00:08:09,451
Byte pair encoding is beautifully simple.

91
00:08:09,451 --> 00:08:11,339
Start with single characters.

92
00:08:11,339 --> 00:08:13,032
Count every adjacent pair.

93
00:08:13,032 --> 00:08:18,372
Merge the most frequent pair into a single new token and add it to the vocabulary.

94
00:08:18,372 --> 00:08:21,692
Repeat until you reach your target vocabulary size.

95
00:08:21,692 --> 00:08:32,110
It was invented by Philip Gage in nineteen ninety four as a compression trick, and Sennrich and colleagues adapted it for machine translation in twenty fifteen.

96
00:08:32,110 --> 00:08:35,496
GPT-2 then made a byte level version of it standard.

97
00:08:36,346 --> 00:08:42,229
Here's BPE on the book's toy corpus: low three times, lower once, newest twice.

98
00:08:42,229 --> 00:08:45,506
I computed this in code rather than by hand.

99
00:08:45,506 --> 00:08:49,974
Step one: l and o appear together four times, so they merge.

100
00:08:49,974 --> 00:08:53,548
Step two: lo plus w, also four times, gives low.

101
00:08:53,548 --> 00:08:57,793
Now every remaining pair appears twice, so there's a tie.

102
00:08:57,793 --> 00:09:01,367
My code picks the first pair it finds, n plus e.

103
00:09:01,367 --> 00:09:03,154
The book picks e plus s.

104
00:09:03,154 --> 00:09:11,867
Both are valid; real implementations need a deterministic tie break rule, and different libraries use different ones.

105
00:09:11,867 --> 00:09:18,345
After a few more merges, newest becomes one token while lower stays as low plus pieces.

106
00:09:19,196 --> 00:09:22,198
BPE won because it balances everything.

107
00:09:22,198 --> 00:09:26,432
Tens of thousands of pieces cover practically any text.

108
00:09:26,432 --> 00:09:34,207
A byte level version, like GPT-2's, can always fall back to raw bytes, so there are no true unknowns.

109
00:09:34,207 --> 00:09:36,825
Frequent words are a single token.

110
00:09:36,825 --> 00:09:41,367
And useful prefixes and suffixes emerge just from counting.

111
00:09:41,367 --> 00:09:48,681
There are close cousins: WordPiece in BERT, and the Unigram model in SentencePiece, used by T5.

112
00:09:48,681 --> 00:09:52,299
LLaMA one and two use SentencePiece's BPE mode.

113
00:09:53,149 --> 00:09:53,951
Part five.

114
00:09:53,951 --> 00:09:57,800
Let's train a real BPE tokenizer on Shakespeare.

115
00:09:58,650 --> 00:10:02,809
We use Hugging Face's tokenizers library, written in Rust.

116
00:10:02,809 --> 00:10:16,364
A BPE model, a whitespace pre tokenizer so merges never cross word boundaries, and a trainer with a vocabulary of one thousand, a minimum pair frequency of two, and our four special tokens.

117
00:10:16,364 --> 00:10:24,612
TinyShakespeare is just over one point one million characters, and training took four tenths of a second on my CPU.

118
00:10:25,462 --> 00:10:29,089
Now the most important correction in this chapter.

119
00:10:29,089 --> 00:10:36,199
The book shows twelve tokens, with question as a single token, and says the round trip is perfect.

120
00:10:36,199 --> 00:10:42,003
In the actual run, question splits into que, st and ion, giving fourteen tokens.

121
00:10:42,003 --> 00:10:48,822
And the round trip is false: the decoded text has spaces before the comma and inside question.

122
00:10:48,822 --> 00:10:56,004
That's because the whitespace pre tokenizer throws away spacing information and no decoder was set.

123
00:10:56,004 --> 00:11:02,170
Which is exactly why GPT-2 uses byte level BPE that keeps the space inside the token.

124
00:11:02,170 --> 00:11:05,072
Always run the round trip test yourself.

125
00:11:05,922 --> 00:11:07,888
Here's my fix, my own code.

126
00:11:07,888 --> 00:11:14,658
Swap the whitespace pre tokenizer for a byte level one and set a matching byte level decoder.

127
00:11:14,658 --> 00:11:23,612
Now the space is stored inside the token, shown as a G with a dot in front of be, and decoding reproduces the text exactly.

128
00:11:23,612 --> 00:11:31,693
That's the GPT-2 approach, and because every byte is in the starting alphabet, no unknown token is ever needed.

129
00:11:32,543 --> 00:11:35,466
Now modern text that Shakespeare never wrote.

130
00:11:35,466 --> 00:11:36,700
Twenty five tokens.

131
00:11:36,700 --> 00:11:43,974
Ing is a single token, because Shakespeare is full of ing words, and hat is a token, so Chat becomes C plus hat.

132
00:11:43,974 --> 00:11:47,546
But ChatGPT, neural and network shatter into fragments.

133
00:11:47,546 --> 00:11:53,327
The book's shorter version of this example prints fourteen tokens; my run gives eighteen.

134
00:11:53,327 --> 00:11:58,783
The lesson is right though: a tokenizer is only as good as the text it learned from.

135
00:11:59,633 --> 00:12:04,672
The book says the lowest IDs hold the most frequent characters, e, t, o, a.

136
00:12:04,672 --> 00:12:11,256
In a real run, the starting alphabet is simply sorted: punctuation, then capitals, then lowercase.

137
00:12:11,256 --> 00:12:16,429
Only the merged tokens after the alphabet are in the order they were learned.

138
00:12:16,429 --> 00:12:28,858
And the longest tokens aren't common English words; they're character names like Bolingbroke and Buckingham, because every speech in the play starts with the speaker's name in capitals.

139
00:12:28,858 --> 00:12:34,166
That's a nice data lesson from chapter seven: your formatting gets learned too.

140
00:12:35,016 --> 00:12:46,551
I trained four BPE tokenizers on the same Shakespeare file with vocabularies of five hundred, one thousand, two thousand and five thousand, then tokenized the whole file.

141
00:12:46,551 --> 00:12:59,307
Characters per token rose from about two and a half to almost three point nine, and the token count fell from four hundred and forty eight thousand to two hundred and eighty nine thousand.

142
00:12:59,307 --> 00:13:02,632
Character level would be one character per token.

143
00:13:02,632 --> 00:13:08,332
Bigger vocabularies mean shorter sequences, at the cost of a bigger embedding table.

144
00:13:09,182 --> 00:13:11,629
A trained tokenizer is an artifact.

145
00:13:11,629 --> 00:13:15,754
Save it to JSON, load it back, and check the outputs match.

146
00:13:15,754 --> 00:13:25,751
My file was about sixty thousand bytes; the book's was thirty eight thousand, and the size depends on the library version and the exact merges.

147
00:13:25,751 --> 00:13:30,784
The key point: a model's embedding table is tied to one exact tokenizer.

148
00:13:30,784 --> 00:13:34,419
Lose or change it, and the model's IDs mean nothing.

149
00:13:35,270 --> 00:13:35,884
Part six.

150
00:13:35,884 --> 00:13:39,024
The production tokenizers you'll actually use.

151
00:13:39,874 --> 00:13:43,062
Tiktoken is OpenAI's tokenizer library.

152
00:13:43,062 --> 00:13:49,683
The cl100k base encoding is the one used by GPT-4 and GPT three point five turbo.

153
00:13:49,683 --> 00:13:59,655
Hello, world, how are you becomes eight tokens, and notice the spaces live at the start of tokens: space world, space how.

154
00:13:59,655 --> 00:14:03,824
The round trip is perfect, because it's byte level.

155
00:14:03,824 --> 00:14:10,200
Its vocabulary has one hundred thousand two hundred and seventy seven entries.

156
00:14:10,200 --> 00:14:15,922
Newer OpenAI models use a larger encoding called o two hundred k base.

157
00:14:16,772 --> 00:14:19,976
The notebook counts tokens for three prompts.

158
00:14:19,976 --> 00:14:23,039
The real counts are seven, fifteen and ten.

159
00:14:23,039 --> 00:14:26,956
The book prints six, fourteen and nine, each one short.

160
00:14:26,956 --> 00:14:29,377
Haiku, for example, is two tokens.

161
00:14:29,377 --> 00:14:45,117
The same kind of slip appears in the comparison section: the Hamlet line is twelve tokens in GPT-4's tokenizer, not eleven, and the backpropagation sentence is nine, with backpropagation split into back, prop and agation.

162
00:14:45,117 --> 00:14:50,387
Ironically, the book's own advice is the right one: don't assume, measure.

163
00:14:51,237 --> 00:14:57,161
Hugging Face's auto tokenizer loads the tokenizer that matches any model on the hub.

164
00:14:57,161 --> 00:15:07,317
For GPT-2, the same sentence splits into the same eight pieces, but the IDs are completely different, because each model has its own vocabulary.

165
00:15:07,317 --> 00:15:12,253
You also get an attention mask: one for real tokens, zero for padding.

166
00:15:12,253 --> 00:15:15,216
Here there's no padding, so it's all ones.

167
00:15:16,066 --> 00:15:19,166
Here are some real vocabulary sizes.

168
00:15:19,166 --> 00:15:24,334
GPT-2 and GPT-3: fifty thousand two hundred and fifty seven.

169
00:15:24,334 --> 00:15:28,210
GPT-4's encoding: about one hundred thousand.

170
00:15:28,210 --> 00:15:31,224
GPT-4o: about two hundred thousand.

171
00:15:31,224 --> 00:15:36,909
BERT: thirty thousand five hundred and twenty two, with WordPiece.

172
00:15:36,909 --> 00:15:45,866
LLaMA one and two: thirty two thousand, and LLaMA three jumped to one hundred and twenty eight thousand.

173
00:15:45,866 --> 00:15:50,948
The trend is clear: bigger vocabularies, shorter sequences.

174
00:15:50,948 --> 00:15:54,652
I added the GPT-4o and LLaMA 3 rows myself.

175
00:15:55,502 --> 00:15:56,250
Part seven.

176
00:15:56,250 --> 00:15:59,448
Tokenizer quirks that affect prompts and costs.

177
00:16:00,298 --> 00:16:02,311
Quirk one: the leading space.

178
00:16:02,311 --> 00:16:08,280
Hello and space Hello are different tokens with different IDs, and the same for world.

179
00:16:08,280 --> 00:16:15,289
Because most words in running text follow a space, BPE learns the spaced version as the common token.

180
00:16:15,289 --> 00:16:24,450
So Translate colon Hello and Translate colon space Hello give the same number of tokens, but the model sees a different final token.

181
00:16:24,450 --> 00:16:26,880
Spacing in prompts is not cosmetic.

182
00:16:27,730 --> 00:16:28,994
Quirk two: numbers.

183
00:16:28,994 --> 00:16:40,174
In cl100k, digits are grouped into chunks of at most three, so ten thousand becomes one hundred, then zero zero, and twenty twenty four becomes two zero two, then four.

184
00:16:40,174 --> 00:16:46,828
In my run, the answer is ten thousand in digits takes seven tokens, while spelling it out takes six.

185
00:16:46,828 --> 00:16:55,612
Splits like this are one reason models struggle with arithmetic: the same digit sits in different positions inside different tokens.

186
00:16:56,462 --> 00:16:59,159
Quirk three, and another correction.

187
00:16:59,159 --> 00:17:05,377
The book says common emoji are single tokens and three fire emoji are three tokens.

188
00:17:05,377 --> 00:17:13,467
In my run, the smiley is two tokens, the rocket and thumbs up are three each, and three fire emoji are nine.

189
00:17:13,467 --> 00:17:21,183
An emoji is four bytes in UTF-8, and byte level BPE only merges the byte sequences it saw often enough.

190
00:17:21,183 --> 00:17:27,101
Interestingly, space smiley inside a sentence is one token, so context matters.

191
00:17:27,101 --> 00:17:33,768
The book also says hello smiley world is four tokens while listing three; three is right.

192
00:17:34,618 --> 00:17:36,650
Quirk four: other languages.

193
00:17:36,650 --> 00:17:51,891
Café is two tokens, naive with a diaeresis is three, the Japanese word for Japanese language is four, with the last character split into two byte pieces, and hello world in Russian is five tokens for two words.

194
00:17:51,891 --> 00:17:58,859
English gets roughly one token per word, while many other languages pay two or three times more.

195
00:17:58,859 --> 00:18:07,786
That means higher cost and less text in the context window, which is why multilingual models train multilingual tokenizers.

196
00:18:08,635 --> 00:18:09,372
Part eight.

197
00:18:09,372 --> 00:18:12,988
Connecting to chapter nine, then exercises and review.

198
00:18:13,837 --> 00:18:17,405
The notebook closes the loop with chapter seven.

199
00:18:17,405 --> 00:18:23,202
Each JSON lines record gets two new fields, its token IDs and its token count.

200
00:18:23,202 --> 00:18:26,695
Three examples average five point seven tokens.

201
00:18:26,695 --> 00:18:29,965
That's exactly the input chapter nine needs.

202
00:18:30,816 --> 00:18:34,375
One last book check, in the preview of chapter nine.

203
00:18:34,375 --> 00:18:39,097
The book creates an embedding layer with a keyword called vocab size.

204
00:18:39,097 --> 00:18:43,546
PyTorch doesn't have that keyword; I ran it and got a type error.

205
00:18:43,546 --> 00:18:49,090
The parameter is called num embeddings, or you can pass the numbers positionally.

206
00:18:49,090 --> 00:19:01,546
The result is a table of fifty thousand two hundred and fifty seven rows by seven hundred and sixty eight columns, about thirty eight point six million parameters, one row per token.

207
00:19:01,546 --> 00:19:04,900
That's the cost of vocabulary size made concrete.

208
00:19:05,750 --> 00:19:09,207
Some answers to the exercises, from my own runs.

209
00:19:09,207 --> 00:19:30,449
Exercise one: the sentence has twenty three distinct characters and eighty two characters total; the word tokenizer has thirteen entries including the four special tokens and a sequence of nine words, with no unknowns since it was fit on the same text, and note tokenization keeps its full stop.

210
00:19:30,449 --> 00:19:37,506
Exercise three: estimate cost as input tokens plus maximum output tokens, times the current price.

211
00:19:37,506 --> 00:19:45,570
Exercise four: the leading space changes the ID for every word, and for rarer words it can change the count too.

212
00:19:45,570 --> 00:19:48,667
Exercise five: English is usually cheapest.

213
00:19:49,517 --> 00:19:50,485
Key takeaways.

214
00:19:50,485 --> 00:19:55,185
Tokenization is a reversible mapping, so always test the round trip.

215
00:19:55,185 --> 00:19:59,401
The core tradeoff is vocabulary size against sequence length.

216
00:19:59,401 --> 00:20:02,719
BPE merges the most frequent pair over and over.

217
00:20:02,719 --> 00:20:09,147
Byte level BPE keeps spaces inside tokens, so it's lossless and never needs an unknown token.

218
00:20:09,147 --> 00:20:11,981
Tokenizers depend on their training data.

219
00:20:11,981 --> 00:20:19,170
And quirks with spaces, numbers, emoji and languages change token counts, so measure rather than assume.

